commons bynk.decode {
	---
	Why a decode failed, as `e.kind` on a `DecodeError`: `Malformed` for text that
	is not valid JSON (or YAML), `StructuralMismatch` for a value of the wrong shape,
	`RefinementViolation` for a value that breaks a refinement, and the rest for the
	YAML decoder's refusals. It is closed, so a `match` on it is checked for
	exhaustiveness and a new kind is a visible change; the `_` arm is the way to
	opt out. Every decoder (`Json.decode`, `Json.decodeSql`, `Yaml.decode`,
	`Form.decode`) answers with the same sum.

	`"\(e.kind)"` is the variant's name, and an HTTP `400` body still writes that
	name as text.

	Its variants name a `DecodeErrorKind` only where the context says so (a `match`
	or `is` on one, an expected type, the other side of `==` or `!=`): a bare
	`Malformed` is never a `DecodeErrorKind` on its own, so a type of your own with
	a `Malformed` variant is unaffected. `DecodeErrorKind.Malformed` always works.
	---
	type DecodeErrorKind = enum {
		Malformed,
		StructuralMismatch,
		RefinementViolation,
		UnsupportedAnchor,
		UnsupportedAlias,
		UnsupportedMergeKey,
		UnsupportedTag,
		MultipleDocuments,
		UnsupportedSyntax,
		UnsupportedValue,
		DuplicateKey,
		LimitExceeded,
	}
}
